\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Zernike order selection by BIC. Pixel RMS decreases monotonically with more parameters (from \SI {0.47}{px} at 57~params to \SI {0.41}{px} at 141~params), but BIC penalises complexity. The BIC minimum is at O(1)+d(3) (93~params, BIC~= $-5093$, RMS~= \SI {0.41}{px}), though O(0)+d(2) (57~params, BIC~= $-4522$) is within $\sim $10\,\% in RMS at 40\,\% fewer parameters. The O(0)+d(2) model (57~params, \SI {0.47}{px}) is selected for physical model construction because it is internally consistent with the rigid-sub-pupil CMO assumption, achieves \SI {90}{\percent } of the noise-floor accuracy at nearly half the parameters of the BIC minimum, and the marginal improvement above d(2) falls within corner detection noise.}}{5}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bic_order}{{3}{5}{Zernike order selection by BIC. Pixel RMS decreases monotonically with more parameters (from \SI {0.47}{px} at 57~params to \SI {0.41}{px} at 141~params), but BIC penalises complexity. The BIC minimum is at O(1)+d(3) (93~params, BIC~= $-5093$, RMS~= \SI {0.41}{px}), though O(0)+d(2) (57~params, BIC~= $-4522$) is within $\sim $10\,\% in RMS at 40\,\% fewer parameters. The O(0)+d(2) model (57~params, \SI {0.47}{px}) is selected for physical model construction because it is internally consistent with the rigid-sub-pupil CMO assumption, achieves \SI {90}{\percent } of the noise-floor accuracy at nearly half the parameters of the BIC minimum, and the marginal improvement above d(2) falls within corner detection noise}{figure.caption.3}{}}
